Article 6 Chinese medicine varieties that meet one of the following conditions may apply for Grade I protection:
(1) having special therapeutic effects on specific diseases;
(2) being artificial products equivalent to nationally protected Grade I wild medicinal species;
(3) being used for the prevention and treatment of special diseases.

Article 7 Chinese medicine varieties that meet one of the following conditions may apply for Grade II protection:
(1) varieties meeting the provisions of Article 6 of these Regulations or varieties for which Grade I protection has been removed;
(2) having significant therapeutic effects on specific diseases;
(3) effective substances extracted from natural medicines and special preparations.

Article 18 Where a Chinese medicine variety approved for protection by the drug regulatory department of the State Council was produced by multiple enterprises before approval, the enterprises that did not apply for the "Certificate of Protected Chinese Medicine Variety" shall, within six months from the date of the public announcement, report to the drug regulatory department of the State Council and provide relevant materials in accordance with Article 10 of these Regulations. The drug regulatory department of the State Council shall designate a drug inspection institution to conduct a quality inspection of the same variety for such reported variety. Based on the inspection results, the drug regulatory department of the State Council may take the following measures:
(1) For those meeting the national drug standards, issue the "Certificate of Protected Chinese Medicine Variety" retroactively;
(2) For those not meeting the national drug standards, revoke the approval number of the Chinese medicine variety in accordance with the provisions of the drug administration laws and administrative regulations.
